{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And Elohim said, etc., for the earth is filled with חמס, etc.</b> And if you would say: well, if we interpret His saying חמס as our Sages said (Genesis Rabbah 31) — that it is idolatry and illicit relations, etc. — then there is reason in the matter; but according to one who says that it is literal חמס, namely robbery, why were the robbed punished along with the robbers? Granted that one could say they were all robbers, each according to his ability plundering according to his capability." "Nevertheless, the matter can be explained according to their words (Leviticus Rabbah 23:9), which they stated, and this is their language: 'In a place where you find licentiousness, etc., it kills both the worthy and the wicked' — end of quote. According to these words, Scripture intends to resolve our difficulty: that although a decree was sealed upon robbery, nevertheless, since among those transgressions for which they were judged there was also licentiousness, even those who were not robbers were killed. And if you would say: why did they not forgive one another so that their decree would not be sealed? Perhaps because HaShem did not wish to reveal this secret except to Noah and not to the people of his generation, and Noah cannot say the matter, for whatever he does not say is under the rubric of 'you shall not say' (Yoma 4a). And the reason is that the Holy One, blessed be He, knew they would not be moved by the matter — or perhaps they knew but paid no attention to it, and this is somewhat forced, for why would they not make a rectification that involves no loss of anything. And the main point is that even if they forgave, it would not help, since the law of robbery was upon them and they were liable to death on its account, and forgiveness avails in no manner whatsoever. And it should not trouble you [in light of] the statement of our Sages, of blessed memory, that we cited above regarding what we explained — that there was no liability of death [for robbery alone] — for their intention, of blessed memory, was not that they did not commit robbery besides this, but rather that they committed every form of חמס, and when something came to hand that they could not rob, they would resort to deception in the manner described, and thousands of them would gather to rob a basket of lupines." "<b>And the reason</b> He made known all this to Noah is to command him to make an ark for the preservation of the species." "And one should pay attention to why Noah turned a blind eye to the prospect of performing deeds of righteousness among the people of his generation to seek mercy for them before HaShem. And the difficulty grows even greater when we observe that our great father Abraham learned from him, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 49), that [Abraham] did not seek mercy for fewer than ten because he saw that Noah did not seek, etc. And it is difficult, for who told him that if he had sought, it would not have been accepted?" "<b>However,</b> from the words of YHWH that He spoke to Noah, He revealed to Noah His mind that the matter was decided in the eyes of YHWH to destroy, and He gave Noah no opening to pray — as He said, 'the end of all flesh,' etc., 'and behold I destroy them' — with finality; what room does he have to pray? This is unlike when He spoke His words to Abraham, where we do not find that He spoke in such terms, but rather gave him room to pray, as it is written (Genesis 18:21): 'אֵרְדָה נָּא וְאֶרְאֶה הַכְּצַעֲקָתָהּ,' etc. And although in truth the decree was issued there as well, nevertheless HaShem did what He intended in order to show Abraham His way of conduct, and as we explained in its place, and He said things from which Abraham would hear that he had permission to seek mercy. And so you will find in His speaking to Moses in the reproofs for the sin of Israel, He did not finalize the decree — and thereby Moses found room to pray, as we explained in its place (Numbers 14:11) — whereas with Noah, HaShem closed off his prayer." "<b>And furthermore</b>, he [Noah] had no opening such as Abraham and Moses had, whose reason and justification were with them — namely, that there was no compelling need for HaShem to inform them, which itself points clearly to the fact that He was asking them to pray. Not so with Noah: there is no room to ask why HaShem informed him, for there is a reason in the matter — that he must prepare an ark and its needs — and additionally He had already decreed the verdict irrevocably; therefore he did not pray. And from the episode one learns regarding Abraham: since HaShem did not give him an opening to pray, infer from this that fewer than ten would not suffice."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:55.548112165Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}